<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/intermediate/the-four-fields">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Anthropology's four fields — <strong>cultural, archaeological, biological and
    linguistic</strong> — cover the human story from every angle: living societies, past
    societies, evolving bodies, and language in use. The division is American (Boas); the
    integration is the point: no other discipline spans australopithecines to Zoom calls.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four fields and what each contributes" widget="equationStep" config={fieldsCfg} />

  <h2>History that shaped the discipline</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Boas dismantled scientific racism (early 1900s):</strong> his immigrant-head-measurement studies showed skull shape tracked environment, not fixed racial type — anthropology's founding anti-racist contribution, decades ahead of genetics' confirmation.</li>
    <li><strong>Mead and Benedict humanised the field:</strong> culture-and-personality studies (Coming of Age in Samoa, 1928) made cultural variation readable to millions — with later critiques (Freeman) teaching the field methodological humility in public.</li>
    <li><strong>The crisis of representation (1980s):</strong> "Writing Culture" (1986) asked who gets to speak for whom — the reflexive turn made the anthropologist's position part of the data, not a hidden narrator.</li>
    <li><strong>The holistic bet:</strong> four fields together is anthropology's wager — that genes, artefacts, words and lives only make sense in one frame. Whatever you study, the other three fields are the context.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Boas's writings; Mead (1928); Clifford & Marcus, Writing Culture (1986); Kuper, Anthropology and Anthropologists.</p>
</TopicPage>
